xnu 0279: copy the frame at the display tick, not on the renderer's thread

`late avg 8 ms max 16.5 ms` (ring244) is the phase at which frames reached the
panel, and the panel's own refresh quantised that jitter into one or two
periods: the copy happened wherever the compositor finished, so the same frame
could be shown 8 ms or 16 ms after its predecessor even at a steady rate.

`swap()` now only records the request; the vsync callback presents it, after
the clients have been notified and after the timer is armed for the next tick.
Two things follow: every copy starts at the same point in the period, and the
2.8 ms the copy costs leaves the renderer's thread -- which is where the 46-56
fps of a drag was being lost. The presentation rate and its cost are printed as
`present N/s avg/max`, and `mondrianfb_defer=0` restores the old path.

